1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:02,240
Cent #9344.

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:06,519
A penny struck in 1974

3
00:00:06,559 --> 00:00:07,559
with no mint mark,

4
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:09,939
generally indicating the Philadelphia mint.

5
00:00:10,679 --> 00:00:12,740
It carries nine of the nineteen traits

6
00:00:12,779 --> 00:00:15,580
in the CENTS Traits Atlas, more than any other Cent.

7
00:00:16,019 --> 00:00:17,179
No Cent carries eight.

8
00:00:17,559 --> 00:00:18,679
Three carry seven.

9
00:00:20,199 --> 00:00:23,719
Faceless. Corroded. Orange.

10
00:00:24,780 --> 00:00:27,199
Significant Verdigris, Obsidian and Red:

11
00:00:27,539 --> 00:00:29,920
each a major visual actor, short of Full.

12
00:00:30,780 --> 00:00:31,559
Notable Gold.

13
00:00:32,579 --> 00:00:33,560
Notable Abraded:

14
00:00:33,899 --> 00:00:35,579
chunky marks where metal is missing.

15
00:00:36,359 --> 00:00:38,899
And Blood: a residue named for how it looks,

16
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:40,619
found on seventeen Cents.

17
00:00:43,299 --> 00:00:45,119
Beside Cent #851,

18
00:00:45,359 --> 00:00:47,380
struck the same year and close to mint state,

19
00:00:47,740 --> 00:00:49,560
every one of those marks stands out.

20
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:52,359
No one chose these marks.

21
00:00:53,219 --> 00:00:54,740
Chang catalogued each penny

22
00:00:54,759 --> 00:00:56,079
by its year and mint mark.

23
00:00:56,899 --> 00:00:59,500
Of his work in general, Chang said in 2017:

24
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:00,880
"I'm not making aesthetic

25
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:02,340
choices during the process.

26
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:03,380
I don't know how to.

27
00:01:03,740 --> 00:01:05,420
I don't trust my own aesthetic choice."

28
00:01:06,540 --> 00:01:07,819
In the words of Sol LeWitt,

29
00:01:07,859 --> 00:01:11,099
in 1967: "The serial artist does not attempt

30
00:01:11,140 --> 00:01:13,579
to produce a beautiful or mysterious object

31
00:01:13,760 --> 00:01:15,739
but functions as a clerk cataloguing

32
00:01:15,759 --> 00:01:17,079
the results of the premise."

33
00:01:18,159 --> 00:01:19,140
Through LeWitt's lens,

34
00:01:19,299 --> 00:01:21,700
the premise is ten thousand copper pennies,

35
00:01:21,939 --> 00:01:24,859
and Cent #9344 is one of its results,

36
00:01:25,239 --> 00:01:27,540
photographed and logged like every other Cent,

37
00:01:27,739 --> 00:01:29,000
whatever time had done to it.

38
00:01:30,099 --> 00:01:32,780
In the words of Boris Groys, in his book On the New:

39
00:01:33,180 --> 00:01:35,420
"a particularly powerful aura surrounds

40
00:01:35,459 --> 00:01:37,719
precisely those works in which the highest

41
00:01:37,840 --> 00:01:40,200
possible cultural aspirations are combined

42
00:01:40,239 --> 00:01:41,379
with the most profane,

43
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:43,700
insignificant, valueless things."

44
00:01:44,879 --> 00:01:47,179
CENTS inscribed images of the humblest

45
00:01:47,239 --> 00:01:48,558
of coins on Bitcoin,

46
00:01:48,879 --> 00:01:50,959
and carried their copper into the galleries

47
00:01:51,019 --> 00:01:52,539
of UCCA Beijing.

48
00:01:53,540 --> 00:01:54,900
A Cent's worth is no longer

49
00:01:55,019 --> 00:01:56,859
set by its metal or its face.

50
00:01:57,879 --> 00:02:01,000
The nine traits of Cent #9344 were named

51
00:02:01,060 --> 00:02:02,799
after the penny itself was melted,

52
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:04,799
by collectors and the Trait Council.

53
00:02:05,560 --> 00:02:07,579
Its value is still being redefined,

54
00:02:07,760 --> 00:02:10,039
again and again, by the people taking part.

55
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:14,620
Cent #9344, a great Cent.
